210
00:17:01,230 --> 00:17:05,348
one foundational concept, which is tokenization.

211
00:17:06,830 --> 00:17:07,908
So what is tokenization?

212
00:17:09,294 --> 00:17:10,692
So when we talk about text,

213
00:17:12,206 --> 00:17:15,908
text is not really something that models can understand.

214
00:17:16,432 --> 00:17:18,660
Models understand numbers,

215
00:17:19,534 --> 00:17:20,548
they don't understand text.

216
00:17:22,574 --> 00:17:32,868
So the first goal is to find a way to represent that text in forms of something that models can understand, so in forms of numbers.

217
00:17:34,286 --> 00:17:37,924
So the way that we do that is we first try

218
00:17:38,702 --> 00:17:39,844
to divide the text

219
00:17:40,992 --> 00:17:44,516
into entities that we can then represent.

220
00:17:46,190 --> 00:17:50,468
And so the process of dividing the text into indivisible entities

221
00:17:51,118 --> 00:17:52,676
is what is called tokenization.

222
00:17:53,678 --> 00:17:57,124
So in this example, we have a cute teddy bear is reading,

223
00:17:58,352 --> 00:18:03,552
and one possible tokenization is to say that A

224
00:18:04,622 --> 00:18:13,232
is one indivisible unit, cute is another unit, teddy bear can be another unit, and so on and so forth.

225
00:18:15,182 --> 00:18:16,676
So each of these elements

226
00:18:18,702 --> 00:18:20,004
is called a token,

227
00:18:21,326 --> 00:18:22,276
and our goal

228
00:18:23,534 --> 00:18:25,284
is to represent these tokens

229
00:18:26,094 --> 00:18:29,508
with mathematical representations that are meaningful.

230
00:18:30,862 --> 00:18:34,340
So in this case, we want each token to be represented by a vector,

231
00:18:35,502 --> 00:18:36,420
a vector of numbers.

232
00:18:40,238 --> 00:18:40,512
So

233
00:18:42,414 --> 00:18:45,796
in order for you to tokenize an input text,

234
00:18:46,542 --> 00:18:51,556
you need to know what are the tokens that are allowed for you to be using.

235
00:18:52,110 --> 00:18:54,756
And so we have something that's called a vocabulary,

236
00:18:55,712 --> 00:18:58,788
which includes all possible tokens that you can use.

237
00:19:00,718 --> 00:19:03,332
And depending on the vocabulary that you choose,

238
00:19:04,512 --> 00:19:05,924
you may end up

239
00:19:06,752 --> 00:19:09,050
with different sequence length.

240
00:19:09,550 --> 00:19:12,676
So here by sequence length I mean the number of tokens

241
00:19:13,326 --> 00:19:19,012
by which you divided your text. So for instance, in this instance, we have one, two, three, four, five, six.

242
00:19:19,598 --> 00:19:20,164
Six tokens.

243
00:19:21,774 --> 00:19:22,992
We're going to see in a second

244
00:19:23,822 --> 00:19:25,188
that there's a tight

245
00:19:26,062 --> 00:19:29,188
relationship between vocabulary size and sequence length.

246
00:19:29,552 --> 00:19:31,472
And that's something that will inform

247
00:19:32,302 --> 00:19:35,152
which tokenizer we should be choosing.

248
00:19:38,126 --> 00:19:38,820
So far so good?

249
00:19:39,790 --> 00:19:45,028
So now you may be wondering well, all of that is great, but how do I know

250
00:19:45,998 --> 00:19:47,232
how I tokenize my text?

251
00:19:47,392 --> 00:19:51,972
Like this is just a theoretical concept, but how do I do that?

252
00:19:52,192 --> 00:19:59,012
So one natural way is for you to say, okay, so I'll just tokenize the text

253
00:19:59,726 --> 00:20:00,992
with respect to words.

254
00:20:01,262 --> 00:20:05,252
So I'm going to consider space as being a delimiter between

255
00:20:06,382 --> 00:20:06,884
tokens.

256
00:20:07,534 --> 00:20:12,292
So here I would say a cute teddy bear is reading, so every word in that text would actually represent

257
00:20:13,422 --> 00:20:13,988
a token.

258
00:20:16,238 --> 00:20:18,832
So what are the pros and cons of this approach?

259
00:20:19,054 --> 00:20:19,652
So pros,

260
00:20:20,782 --> 00:20:23,140
very simple, especially for the English language.

261
00:20:23,792 --> 00:20:27,172
You map a token to its words, very simple.

262
00:20:27,726 --> 00:20:38,724
It is also interpretable as in you know when you get a representation of a word that it corresponds, or a representation of a token that it corresponds to a given word.

263
00:20:40,590 --> 00:20:41,232
However,

264
00:20:42,862 --> 00:20:44,004
at least in English,

265
00:20:45,312 --> 00:20:47,716
you have many variations of words.

266
00:20:48,206 --> 00:20:49,060
So for instance,

267
00:20:50,702 --> 00:20:57,732
a noun like you can have it as singular and plural, you can have the masculine form, the feminine form.

268
00:20:58,606 --> 00:21:00,196
You can have many, many variations.

269
00:21:00,832 --> 00:21:04,912
So your vocabulary size may get very large as a result of that.

270
00:21:05,230 --> 00:21:05,924
So that's one con.

271
00:21:07,438 --> 00:21:08,708
The second con is

272
00:21:09,678 --> 00:21:14,628
we're going to see in a second that when we learn the representation of each token,

273
00:21:16,398 --> 00:21:31,044
there is nothing that guarantees us that two tokens that represent the same thing would get the same representation. So for instance, if you consider the word bear and the word bears,

274
00:21:32,142 --> 00:21:35,908
these are the singular and plural of the same animal, which is a bear.

275
00:21:36,750 --> 00:21:45,152
And so you wouldn't want the representation of these two tokens to be too far from one another because they represent the same thing.

276
00:21:46,222 --> 00:21:49,156
Well, with this way of tokenizing your text,

277
00:21:50,272 --> 00:21:53,604
you would have a hard time, first of all,

278
00:21:54,350 --> 00:22:02,596
leveraging the knowledge that you have from the similar token, and second, just guaranteeing that these representations are similar.

279
00:22:04,430 --> 00:22:05,924
And then the third one is,

280
00:22:07,152 --> 00:22:12,004
well, given that you need to learn the representation of each of these

281
00:22:12,782 --> 00:22:13,232
words.

282
00:22:13,422 --> 00:22:14,916
so one word is one token,

283
00:22:15,872 --> 00:22:17,572
you may run into a case

284
00:22:18,752 --> 00:22:19,072
where

285
00:22:20,270 --> 00:22:22,756
if you train your tokenizer on a given

286
00:22:23,694 --> 00:22:24,580
corpus of text,

287
00:22:25,678 --> 00:22:30,468
that you actually do not have a given word that you then encounter

288
00:22:31,438 --> 00:22:32,196
at inference time.

289
00:22:33,294 --> 00:22:43,268
So you may actually be missing representations that you could not have had the chance to learn just because you did not encounter that word at training time.

290
00:22:44,270 --> 00:22:47,460
And therefore, you can run into the risk of

291
00:22:49,262 --> 00:22:51,172
out of vocabulary, meaning that

292
00:22:52,302 --> 00:22:53,072
a token

293
00:22:54,510 --> 00:22:59,012
is actually not something that you have seen at training time, and you don't have the representation for that one.

294
00:23:00,814 --> 00:23:01,892
So, long story short,

295
00:23:02,830 --> 00:23:07,748
word-level tokenization is simple, but it comes with a few cons.

296
00:23:08,912 --> 00:23:17,956
And one of the cons is really the fact that you are not able to leverage words of similar roots,

297
00:23:18,350 --> 00:23:22,244
and this leads to vocabulary being large and risk of

298
00:23:22,926 --> 00:23:23,780
out of vocabulary.

299
00:23:27,726 --> 00:23:30,496
So the next question is okay, can we do better?

300
00:23:30,670 --> 00:23:34,884
Is there another way of tokenizing the text that could make more sense?

301
00:23:35,856 --> 00:23:36,176
So

302
00:23:37,166 --> 00:23:38,820
a natural thought could be

303
00:23:39,536 --> 00:23:43,780
well, you don't divide by words, you divide by character.

304
00:23:45,134 --> 00:23:50,308
You have a fixed number of allowed characters, at least in English or in any languages.

305
00:23:51,214 --> 00:23:51,936
And so

306
00:23:53,456 --> 00:23:54,736
you are not

307
00:23:55,534 --> 00:24:04,260
running the risk of out of vocabulary anymore because you will encounter the all the letters in the alphabet in your training set, or you can try to do that.

308
00:24:05,456 --> 00:24:08,644
And by doing so, you will also have a small vocabulary.

309
00:24:10,016 --> 00:24:18,016
And you would also be robust to casing and misspellings that people usually make in

310
00:24:18,336 --> 00:24:21,508
when I mean, I'm not sure if you recognize yourself. I do.

311
00:24:21,774 --> 00:24:30,416
So when I type I don't know on my computer, I sometimes misspell things, and I wouldn't want the representation of a word

312
00:24:30,702 --> 00:24:34,532
I meant to write to be different from the actual word.

313
00:24:35,278 --> 00:24:39,172
And so, here, we would not be going into the case of

314
00:24:41,006 --> 00:24:43,588
not recognizing a word just because it is misspelled,

315
00:24:45,902 --> 00:24:46,176
but

316
00:24:47,342 --> 00:24:48,612
we will see that

317
00:24:49,678 --> 00:24:50,692
if you do that,

318
00:24:51,630 --> 00:24:52,740
the sequence length

319
00:24:53,838 --> 00:24:56,388
is going to get much longer.

320
00:24:58,416 --> 00:25:03,268
And model complexity is a function of sequence length.

321
00:25:04,334 --> 00:25:09,796
So if the sequence is very long, then your model will take a long time to process the sequence.

322
00:25:10,414 --> 00:25:13,476
So it will make computations much slower.

323
00:25:14,896 --> 00:25:15,216
And

324
00:25:16,174 --> 00:25:23,936
the embeddings themselves, they're much less interpretable, as in, you know, if we tell you the letter A has this representation,

325
00:25:24,814 --> 00:25:29,316
it's maybe much more abstract than talking about word representation.

326
00:25:31,150 --> 00:25:32,976
And we will see that in a second.

327
00:25:36,430 --> 00:25:38,176
So far, does that make sense?

328
00:25:39,086 --> 00:25:39,492
Yeah?

329
00:25:40,974 --> 00:25:42,020
So I hope

330
00:25:44,142 --> 00:25:53,124
I am motivating trade-off enough that the next natural thought in your mind is to actually try something in between, so not word, not character,

331
00:25:53,486 --> 00:25:55,236
but in between, so subwords.

332
00:25:55,886 --> 00:25:56,896
So, how about

333
00:25:57,870 --> 00:25:59,044
we tokenize the text

334
00:26:00,576 --> 00:26:02,436
by leveraging

335
00:26:03,456 --> 00:26:07,620
knowledge that we have about words that we can learn from

336
00:26:08,750 --> 00:26:10,404
some training corpus,

337
00:26:11,296 --> 00:26:18,256
and we divide common occurrences of letters, and we'll represent them with a token.

338
00:26:18,416 --> 00:26:19,588
So for instance, for

339
00:26:20,398 --> 00:26:25,284
reading, we would represent read with a token and ink with another token.

340
00:26:26,542 --> 00:26:27,588
So how about we would do that?

341
00:26:28,910 --> 00:26:31,908
So the good thing here is that we would leverage the root of words.

342
00:26:32,238 --> 00:26:39,876
So in my previous example, bear and bears would have the, for instance, bear token in common.

343
00:26:41,518 --> 00:26:44,932
And then the second thing is it would be actually learned from the data,

344
00:26:45,678 --> 00:26:51,524
as in the way you tokenize text is informed by how words are actually written.

345
00:26:53,518 --> 00:26:56,176
But then the con is, well, you need to do that extra work.

346
00:26:56,334 --> 00:27:00,580
So in the word level case, you just need to produce tokens by

347
00:27:01,678 --> 00:27:03,456
just dividing based on space.

348
00:27:03,616 --> 00:27:07,364
So here you do you need to do some extra work, and then

349
00:27:09,454 --> 00:27:12,964
the way you obtain these tokens will be highly dependent on

350
00:27:14,926 --> 00:27:18,016
the type of data that you have when you do your

351
00:27:19,118 --> 00:27:20,196
tokenizer training.

352
00:27:20,430 --> 00:27:22,176
So that's another thing, another con.

353
00:27:22,670 --> 00:27:25,060
So we need to make sure that the data that you use

354
00:27:25,806 --> 00:27:27,456
to learn how to tokenize

355
00:27:27,456 --> 00:27:30,980
things is actually aligned with your end goal.

356
00:27:35,726 --> 00:27:35,952
So

357
00:27:37,198 --> 00:27:40,032
I just want to say something here.

358
00:27:40,432 --> 00:27:43,712
So I talked about three ways of tokenizing text.

359
00:27:43,952 --> 00:27:45,060
So word level,

360
00:27:45,742 --> 00:27:48,132
character level, subword level.

361
00:27:48,942 --> 00:27:55,812
Among the three, subword level is by far the most popular nowadays for all the reasons that I mentioned.

362
00:27:57,006 --> 00:28:01,732
And you'll hear the word or the acronym BPE a lot.

363
00:28:02,510 --> 00:28:09,060
So BPE is a type of subword tokenizer that stands for byte pair encoding.

364
00:28:09,838 --> 00:28:16,708
So the paper is linked at the bottom of this slide in case you're interested to learn more, but I'll give the just the gist of how it works.

365
00:28:17,646 --> 00:28:18,532
So BPE

366
00:28:19,632 --> 00:28:20,324
learns

367
00:28:21,134 --> 00:28:23,620
what tokens should be in your vocabulary

368
00:28:24,462 --> 00:28:27,268
by starting from an initial set

369
00:28:28,142 --> 00:28:28,740
of tokens

370
00:28:29,872 --> 00:28:30,192
and

371
00:28:32,112 --> 00:28:34,468
identifying the most frequent pairs

372
00:28:36,112 --> 00:28:37,540
in your training data.

373
00:28:38,542 --> 00:28:41,188
and by coming up with dedicated tokens

374
00:28:42,272 --> 00:28:42,592
for

375
00:28:43,726 --> 00:28:45,316
merged tokens of these pairs.

376
00:28:45,486 --> 00:28:47,072
So for instance, I'll give an example.

377
00:28:47,278 --> 00:28:50,756
Let's imagine your initial vocabulary is all the letters

378
00:28:51,566 --> 00:28:52,260
in the alphabet,

379
00:28:53,166 --> 00:28:55,312
and you have, I don't know, the word

380
00:28:56,592 --> 00:28:56,752
un,

381
00:28:58,126 --> 00:28:59,524
like unidea, and

382
00:29:01,582 --> 00:29:02,404
an amazing course.

383
00:29:02,798 --> 00:29:14,148
So un that occurs very frequently. So here it will say, okay, A and N appear very frequently together. So let's actually add a token that merges the two.

384
00:29:15,950 --> 00:29:22,052
It will add that new token in the vocabulary and then repeat the process until reaching

385
00:29:23,374 --> 00:29:25,924
a desired final vocabulary size.

386
00:29:27,086 --> 00:29:29,476
And that will be your final vocabulary.

387
00:29:31,182 --> 00:29:42,564
So the nice thing is that these tokens are learned from the data, so things that appear together are actually identified and added to your vocabulary. So this will lead to your sequence length

388
00:29:44,014 --> 00:29:48,388
being shorter, so you will not have to decompose your text into that many tokens.

389
00:29:49,006 --> 00:29:50,436
So your model will not take

390
00:29:52,142 --> 00:29:54,352
that much time to process your

391
00:29:54,352 --> 00:29:59,428
text. And so that's where the efficiency comes from. So if you have a training corpus

392
00:30:00,174 --> 00:30:03,812
that has, let's say, a lot of the words that you encounter in practice,

393
00:30:04,752 --> 00:30:08,324
then the sequence length of the tokens, you will get

394
00:30:09,038 --> 00:30:10,180
from a piece of text

395
00:30:11,054 --> 00:30:14,244
that uses the same words as what you had in your training. corpus

396
00:30:15,182 --> 00:30:17,508
would be much more, much shorter.

397
00:30:19,726 --> 00:30:21,796
So in case you're doing multilingual

398
00:30:23,278 --> 00:30:30,532
work, then make sure that your tokenizer is trained on a corpus that represents all the languages that you're interested

399
00:30:30,832 --> 00:30:33,072
in. That's one example.

400
00:30:35,312 --> 00:30:35,632
Okay.

401
00:30:36,398 --> 00:30:37,312
So that was a lot.

402
00:30:37,614 --> 00:30:38,948
So I'll just pause for a second.

403
00:30:39,152 --> 00:30:42,272
Is there any questions so far on

404
00:30:43,534 --> 00:30:43,952
this part?

405
00:30:46,192 --> 00:30:46,404
Yes.

406
00:30:47,792 --> 00:30:54,724
So the question is what is the relationship between BP and N-gram? So Ngram is actually something separate.

407
00:30:55,182 --> 00:31:06,832
And this one, yes, to your point, it will learn the tokens from your corpus by merging the most frequent pairs in an iterative way until reaching a final

408
00:31:07,438 --> 00:31:08,900
desired vocabulary size.

409
00:31:10,894 --> 00:31:16,260
So, next natural question is okay, you have your tokens, then now how do you know how to represent them?

410
00:31:16,654 --> 00:31:17,956
Maybe you're asking yourself this.

411
00:31:18,350 --> 00:31:21,152
And we will see that actually in about five

412
00:31:21,152 --> 00:31:24,228
minutes. So, more on that in a bit.

413
00:31:25,262 --> 00:31:32,740
Actually, I'm looking at the time. I don't think I'm on time, so I'll probably move on. But if you have any questions, please feel free to interrupt me.

414
00:31:34,478 --> 00:31:38,020
Tokenization is really the building block of everything we'll see

415
00:31:39,022 --> 00:31:40,612
from here. So, yeah.

416
00:31:42,862 --> 00:31:52,196
Okay, so so far we've seen tokens that represent text that we want to tokenize, but the truth is we also have some special tokens,

417
00:31:52,846 --> 00:31:55,940
and these special tokens are meant to represent

418
00:31:56,718 --> 00:31:59,680
things that we want to express in a sequence.

419
00:32:00,046 --> 00:32:08,676
So one such token is whenever we encounter a term or a piece that is actually not something that is part of our vocabulary.

420
00:32:09,422 --> 00:32:17,092
So the convention is to have a dedicated token that represents this out of vocabulary piece of text.

421
00:32:18,222 --> 00:32:19,876
And it's called the unknown token.

422
00:32:21,006 --> 00:32:22,500
That's one special token.

423
00:32:23,182 --> 00:32:25,028
So give you an example, Teddy Bear.

424
00:32:25,294 --> 00:32:28,196
Imagine if I write it Teddy DI Bear.

425
00:32:29,102 --> 00:32:32,420
And in the training corpus, we've never seen such a spelling.

426
00:32:35,310 --> 00:32:35,920
Then

427
00:32:37,550 --> 00:32:45,840
if your vocabulary is only composed of these four tokens, so teddy, cute, bear, and unknown, then teddy bear will be tokenized as

428
00:32:46,126 --> 00:32:46,948
unknown token

429
00:32:48,110 --> 00:32:48,644
and then bear.

430
00:32:50,318 --> 00:32:51,012
One example.

431
00:32:52,400 --> 00:32:55,840
Another set of special tokens, so

432
00:32:56,846 --> 00:33:05,540
a lot of the times you need to indicate when the sequence is starting, when it is ending, and we'll see how we do that.

433
00:33:05,966 --> 00:33:14,212
So we have two set of special tokens. One is beginning of sequence, BOS, and then end of sequence, EOS.

434
00:33:15,086 --> 00:33:18,404
Those are mostly used when we want to generate text.

435
00:33:19,310 --> 00:33:28,356
So for instance, when you want to start the generation, the convention is to feed your model with the beginning of sequence token,

436
00:33:28,686 --> 00:33:29,840
just to indicate to your

437
00:33:30,640 --> 00:33:32,960
model that you want to generate text.

438
00:33:33,280 --> 00:33:33,600
And

439
00:33:34,478 --> 00:33:43,760
your model will then at the end of its generation just output the end of sequence token to just tell you that its generation is finished.

440
00:33:44,750 --> 00:33:45,764
So this is how it's used.

441
00:33:46,638 --> 00:33:52,164
So what a cute teddy bear would be beginning of sequence, what a cute teddy bear and end of sequence, as an example.

442
00:33:53,134 --> 00:33:58,596
And then the last one that you may encounter is the padding token, and that one is meant

443
00:33:59,520 --> 00:34:04,420
to make your sequences be of a certain size.

444
00:34:05,806 --> 00:34:07,172
So this is sometimes used

445
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:10,084
for efficiency perspectives.

446
00:34:11,374 --> 00:34:13,764
If you want all your sequences to be of the same length,

447
00:34:14,606 --> 00:34:18,276
because we'll see that we can represent that with vectors, matrices, tensors,

448
00:34:19,120 --> 00:34:23,620
and hardware loves things that have the same dimension,

449
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:27,040
then padding can also be used.

450
00:34:28,590 --> 00:34:33,572
But I want to say that this list is by no means exhaustive,

451
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:37,124
and that you also have a lot of

452
00:34:37,902 --> 00:34:40,292
different conventions that people take.

453
00:34:40,750 --> 00:34:46,884
So this way of writing BOS, EOS, this way of writing padding, it's not universal.

454
00:34:48,718 --> 00:34:52,868
So just don't be surprised if you encounter different variations.

455
00:34:53,550 --> 00:34:55,812
And nowadays you have things like

456
00:34:56,494 --> 00:35:03,716
assistance, so you have a user, assistant, and those are also things that people represent with special tokens.

457
00:35:04,654 --> 00:35:06,756
So again, this is not an exhaustive list,

458
00:35:07,440 --> 00:35:16,356
but just know that such tokens exist and they are meant to convey something about the text that the model should understand.

459
00:35:22,286 --> 00:35:22,692
Cool.

460
00:35:24,142 --> 00:35:25,572
That's it for tokenization.

461
00:35:28,078 --> 00:35:28,740
Any questions?

462
00:35:30,062 --> 00:35:30,372
Yes.

463
00:35:31,214 --> 00:35:36,772
The question is what is the order of magnitude of the vocabulary size for LMs right now? So it's a great question.

464
00:35:37,262 --> 00:35:40,804
It is on the order of hundreds of thousands.

465
00:35:41,838 --> 00:35:45,604
So you will see something like hundreds of thousands, two hundred thousand, something like this.

466
00:35:46,382 --> 00:35:52,484
And these are tokenizers that are able to handle both English but also other languages.

467
00:35:54,672 --> 00:36:00,068
And as I mentioned, the most common type of tokenizer nowadays is the BP one.

468
00:36:00,558 --> 00:36:02,404
So yeah, so these are the things that are

469
00:36:03,086 --> 00:36:03,940
good to have in mind.

470
00:36:05,198 --> 00:36:05,412
Yeah.

471
00:36:06,862 --> 00:36:15,012
So the question is having multiple languages be handled by your tokenizer, does that make your tokenizer a bit less of an expert in a given language?

472
00:36:15,342 --> 00:36:22,468
I think it is hard to say, I would say, because there may be some synergies between languages.

473
00:36:22,912 --> 00:36:27,652
So I would say it's hard to say in general, but that being said,

474
00:36:28,366 --> 00:36:30,564
the vocabulary size that you fix

475
00:36:31,726 --> 00:36:34,180
when you run your BP algorithm

476
00:36:34,992 --> 00:36:38,308
will be filled with tokens from different languages.

477
00:36:38,832 --> 00:36:39,952
So you may

478
00:36:40,686 --> 00:36:48,708
get a vocabulary that may lead you to longer sequence length

479
00:36:49,326 --> 00:36:52,292
if you're only interested in one particular language.

480
00:36:52,832 --> 00:36:56,612
So from an efficiency perspective, maybe you'll see something.

481
00:36:57,198 --> 00:37:02,308
But then from a representations perspective, it's not super clear. So one example is

482
00:37:03,792 --> 00:37:09,572
acronyms of organizations that may be something that people talk about in a bunch of languages.

483
00:37:10,126 --> 00:37:13,540
So you may benefit from learning that representation

484
00:37:14,192 --> 00:37:14,512
from

485
00:37:16,110 --> 00:37:18,212
different texts of different languages.

486
00:37:18,752 --> 00:37:19,072
So

487
00:37:19,726 --> 00:37:20,292
pros and cons.

488
00:37:24,462 --> 00:37:24,932
Great.

489
00:37:26,734 --> 00:37:32,484
Cool. So now what we know, what we have learned, is given an input text,

490
00:37:33,198 --> 00:37:38,116
we're able to generate tokens or represent that text with tokens.

491
00:37:38,798 --> 00:37:43,460
So the next natural question is how to represent those tokens.

492
00:37:44,686 --> 00:37:46,244
So this is what we're going to see now.

493
00:37:47,950 --> 00:37:53,124
So the most naive way to do that is to say that each token

494
00:37:53,774 --> 00:37:54,404
is different.

495
00:37:55,790 --> 00:38:01,604
So it's extremely obvious, but you can represent your tokens by what we call one hot encodings.

496
00:38:02,286 --> 00:38:06,372
So here let's say I have the token soft, I can say, okay, it's

497
00:38:07,232 --> 00:38:09,764
the one zero zero vector.

498
00:38:11,054 --> 00:38:19,652
And if you do that for all your tokens, well, the bad thing is they're all going to be orthogonal to each other

499
00:38:20,430 --> 00:38:21,636
if you take the dot product.

500
00:38:23,470 --> 00:38:24,432
So is it a good thing?

501
00:38:28,432 --> 00:38:30,148
Right, no, it's not a good thing.

502
00:38:30,432 --> 00:38:36,548
Because it doesn't tell you anything about what these tokens are and how they're related to each other.

503
00:38:37,038 --> 00:38:37,956
So it's not what you want.

504
00:38:39,630 --> 00:38:40,292
What you want

505
00:38:41,006 --> 00:38:45,540
is to represent these tokens in a way that reflects

506
00:38:46,222 --> 00:38:48,452
how similar they are to one another.

507
00:38:50,382 --> 00:38:51,268
That is what you want.

508
00:38:52,238 --> 00:39:01,220
So, for instance, you want Teddy Bear to be similar to soft, because teddy bears are soft, but maybe book to be maybe independent.

509
00:39:01,870 --> 00:39:05,796
This is what you want to learn. You want to learn meaningful representations.

510
00:39:06,990 --> 00:39:08,420
And by the way, why do you want to

511
00:39:09,646 --> 00:39:13,028
learn meaningful representations? Well, it's because you want to do something with your text.

512
00:39:13,232 --> 00:39:17,508
You need to have it as input into your model in order to do something as output.

513
00:39:18,286 --> 00:39:20,644
So, for instance, generating the next words,

514
00:39:21,870 --> 00:39:23,716
predicting some class. So you need to

515
00:39:24,398 --> 00:39:25,796
understand what is in your text,

516
00:39:27,534 --> 00:39:33,188
which is the reason why what you want is meaningful token representations.

517
00:39:35,630 --> 00:39:35,872
So

518
00:39:36,832 --> 00:39:37,152
this

519
00:39:38,702 --> 00:39:43,680
is what an earlier method called word2vec

520
00:39:43,680 --> 00:39:46,020
tried to achieve.

521
00:39:46,990 --> 00:39:49,552
So here the idea is

522
00:39:52,334 --> 00:39:54,020
To learn these representations

523
00:39:54,830 --> 00:39:56,740
through what we call a proxy task.

524
00:39:57,646 --> 00:40:04,224
So a proxy task is a task that you're trying to learn, but it's not the task that you're trying to learn as an end

525
00:40:04,384 --> 00:40:09,504
goal. It is a task that you try to learn in order to achieve something else.

526
00:40:10,190 --> 00:40:13,284
And here the proxy task is something like

527
00:40:13,966 --> 00:40:18,724
predicting the word that is in between some context words.

528
00:40:18,990 --> 00:40:22,310
So this is the continuous bag of words version of Word2Vec.

529
00:40:23,534 --> 00:40:25,092
Or predicting words

530
00:40:25,870 --> 00:40:30,180
that are surrounding a given word, that is the skipGram version.

531
00:40:31,278 --> 00:40:33,764
So we'll go through an example so that it becomes clear.

532
00:40:34,990 --> 00:40:37,220
So does everyone know what a neural network is?

533
00:40:38,926 --> 00:40:39,300
Yeah?

534
00:40:40,174 --> 00:40:43,332
Okay, so I represented a very basic neural network.

535
00:40:44,302 --> 00:40:46,404
So you can think of it as an input

536
00:40:47,214 --> 00:40:48,900
matrix multiplication to get

537
00:40:50,766 --> 00:40:57,220
a hidden representation of size D, and then again matrix multiplication to go back to size V your vocabulary size.

538
00:40:58,158 --> 00:40:59,012
So let's assume

539
00:41:00,398 --> 00:41:04,484
that you have the sentence a cute teddy bear is reading.

540
00:41:05,614 --> 00:41:12,304
And let's assume, for the sake of simplicity, that your goal is to predict cute

541
00:41:13,262 --> 00:41:14,384
from the word A.

542
00:41:16,590 --> 00:41:20,612
So let's imagine that your proxy task is to predict the next word.

543
00:41:22,190 --> 00:41:23,584
So here, A,

544
00:41:25,422 --> 00:41:27,684
your very naive representation of A

545
00:41:29,038 --> 00:41:30,276
is the one-hot encoding.

546
00:41:31,214 --> 00:41:36,836
So let's assume that you have a vocabulary size of six, and A is your first token.

547
00:41:37,742 --> 00:41:41,732
So you would represent A with 100000.

548
00:41:43,790 --> 00:41:52,228
You pass it through your network, so think of it as matrix multiplication with this vector, you would have a hidden state of size two.

549
00:41:53,038 --> 00:41:55,652
Let's assume two numbers, 0.2 and 0.9.

550
00:41:57,070 --> 00:42:02,372
And then you pass it through your network, so again another matrix multiplication, and then you have predictions

551
00:42:03,278 --> 00:42:04,324
that sum to one,

552
00:42:05,006 --> 00:42:08,548
so you can use something like a softmax to normalize them.

553
00:42:09,584 --> 00:42:11,824
And your goal is

554
00:42:14,384 --> 00:42:14,704
to

555
00:42:16,430 --> 00:42:18,724
have these probabilities be as close

556
00:42:20,384 --> 00:42:24,164
to the label that you have, which is predicting the word cute.

557
00:42:24,462 --> 00:42:31,204
And so cute, let's suppose that it's the second word of your vocabulary, so it is represented by 0, 1, 0, 0, 0.

558
00:42:32,014 --> 00:42:33,124
So your goal is,

559
00:42:34,158 --> 00:42:39,044
like on the slide, for the 0.4 to be as close as 1 as possible.

560
00:42:41,582 --> 00:42:42,788
So the idea is

561
00:42:43,630 --> 00:42:45,092
to have a loss function

562
00:42:45,742 --> 00:42:46,980
that penalizes

563
00:42:48,814 --> 00:42:54,244
the fact of having your probability of a given word being too far from the word that you're trying to predict.

564
00:42:55,022 --> 00:42:56,964
So in case you know the cross-entropy loss,

565
00:42:58,286 --> 00:43:01,572
and then updates the weights of your model

566
00:43:02,382 --> 00:43:03,332
in order to

567
00:43:04,206 --> 00:43:05,220
reflect what you want.

568
00:43:07,022 --> 00:43:11,972
So just so that we're clear, I will just walk through all the quantities that we mentioned.

569
00:43:12,462 --> 00:43:18,532
So here the one hand encoding that represents the token is of size the vocabulary size.

570
00:43:19,822 --> 00:43:20,932
The hidden state,

571
00:43:21,710 --> 00:43:23,716
or also called latent representation,

572
00:43:24,784 --> 00:43:31,812
is of size D, and D is actually a parameter that you can choose, typically much smaller than the vocabulary size.

573
00:43:33,678 --> 00:43:35,620
And then the predicted word probabilities.

574
00:43:36,014 --> 00:43:42,020
Well, you have a probability per token, so it needs to match the vocabulary size, so it's of size V.

575
00:43:42,926 --> 00:43:45,444
And that also matches the size of the one-hot encoding.

576
00:43:46,158 --> 00:43:48,580
And this is where you're able to

577
00:43:50,382 --> 00:43:53,764
try to penalize for deviations from the word that you're trying to predict.

578
00:43:54,958 --> 00:43:56,484
So let's assume you do that.

579
00:43:57,806 --> 00:44:00,484
For A, trying to predict Qt.

580
00:44:01,454 --> 00:44:04,260
You repeat the same thing for Qt for Teddy Bear.

581
00:44:04,942 --> 00:44:08,480
So you go, Qt, you represent it with the 100 encoding.

582
00:44:08,654 --> 00:44:13,188
you pass it through your network. you have a latent representation. and again, probabilities.

583
00:44:13,742 --> 00:44:22,640
you compare that with the actual label that is representing teddy bear, compute the loss, update, the weights, given how far you are from

584
00:44:23,920 --> 00:44:25,988
the prediction from the label,

585
00:44:26,926 --> 00:44:29,348
and you repeat that through your whole corpus.

586
00:44:30,510 --> 00:44:31,812
Well, at the end of this,

587
00:44:33,006 --> 00:44:35,364
what the authors realize

588
00:44:36,526 --> 00:44:41,348
is that the latent representation that you learn in the middle of the network

589
00:44:42,640 --> 00:44:43,908
actually is

590
00:44:45,646 --> 00:44:46,308
making sense.

591
00:44:47,726 --> 00:44:48,356
As in,

592
00:44:50,062 --> 00:44:51,300
you can compute

593
00:44:52,270 --> 00:45:00,676
similarity measures between the learned representations, and you see that representations of similar tokens

594
00:45:01,454 --> 00:45:02,372
are actually similar.

595
00:45:05,006 --> 00:45:13,764
So on this example, so Teddy Bear is soft, what is Persian poetry to art, so another example is Paris is to France, what Berlin is to Germany.

596
00:45:14,062 --> 00:45:15,940
So you have these nice associations

597
00:45:16,718 --> 00:45:18,948
that are actually something that the model learns.

598
00:45:20,910 --> 00:45:23,492
So that gives you meaningful

599
00:45:24,846 --> 00:45:25,924
word representations.

600
00:45:28,046 --> 00:45:29,124
So that's a method

601
00:45:30,030 --> 00:45:36,036
that appeared, I believe, in the early 2010s. I believe it was 2013, if I remember correctly.

602
00:45:36,622 --> 00:45:38,372
So who can tell me what is a

603
00:45:39,120 --> 00:45:40,484
limitation of this approach?

604
00:45:45,198 --> 00:45:49,040
So here, the representation of the words that we learn.

605
00:45:51,502 --> 00:45:55,280
We saw that they end up being meaningful, but is there a

606
00:45:56,942 --> 00:45:58,468
is there a limitation to this approach?

607
00:45:59,040 --> 00:45:59,360
Yeah.

608
00:46:00,240 --> 00:46:03,748
So one answer is that you would have trouble if you

609
00:46:04,462 --> 00:46:11,280
actually are not encountering something that you have seen during your training process. So yes, that is right.

610
00:46:11,440 --> 00:46:15,780
And I think that's right in actually most cases, beyond that case in particular.

611
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:19,300
so that's one. Is there another, yes, another limitation?

612
00:46:20,942 --> 00:46:22,532
So the answer here is

613
00:46:23,440 --> 00:46:29,060
the dimension scales up when the vocabulary size gets bigger. So actually, this is

614
00:46:29,742 --> 00:46:31,268
a limitation of your tokenizer,

615
00:46:32,174 --> 00:46:35,204
because the tokenizer is the one that determines your vocabulary size.

616
00:46:36,400 --> 00:46:36,720
And

617
00:46:37,838 --> 00:46:38,564
but you're right

618
00:46:41,006 --> 00:46:47,044
that this is a limitation, but it's not specific to that approach, as in you will always need to find a representation of

619
00:46:48,174 --> 00:46:50,596
each element in your vocabulary. So

620
00:46:51,598 --> 00:46:52,964
I mean it's a limitation, but

621
00:46:53,710 --> 00:46:55,812
limitation in methods in general.

622
00:46:57,326 --> 00:46:57,760
Yes.

623
00:46:58,190 --> 00:46:58,720
Oh yes.

624
00:46:58,958 --> 00:47:01,636
So answer is words can have multiple meanings.

625
00:47:01,760 --> 00:47:02,372
So exactly.

626
00:47:02,640 --> 00:47:02,852
So

627
00:47:04,590 --> 00:47:06,400
one limitation here is

628
00:47:07,310 --> 00:47:08,868
if the word, let's say cute,

629
00:47:10,958 --> 00:47:11,812
is in this sentence

630
00:47:13,102 --> 00:47:18,020
versus in another sentence, they will both get the same representation.

631
00:47:18,320 --> 00:47:18,640
So

632
00:47:19,790 --> 00:47:21,988
if you say a cute teddy bear in a nice way

633
00:47:22,720 --> 00:47:26,980
versus someone who sarcastically says, Oh, how cute,

634
00:47:28,334 --> 00:47:31,332
they may mean different things between the word behind the word cute.

635
00:47:32,398 --> 00:47:33,920
So I think a very

636
00:47:36,014 --> 00:47:40,740
classic example is bank, so river bank versus going to the bank.

637
00:47:41,198 --> 00:47:43,524
These are the same words but different meaning.

638
00:47:44,110 --> 00:47:46,532
So the problem with this approach is that

639
00:47:47,886 --> 00:47:49,412
the representation of this word

640
00:47:50,318 --> 00:47:54,052
would actually be the same regardless of the sentence that it is in.

641
00:47:54,766 --> 00:47:55,780
So great point.

642
00:47:56,494 --> 00:48:01,220
Another limitation here is that the word order does not matter.

643
00:48:02,446 --> 00:48:07,044
So let's assume we're saying the child is hugging the teddy bear

644
00:48:07,918 --> 00:48:09,892
and the teddy bear is hugging the child.

645
00:48:10,990 --> 00:48:12,708
These are the exact same words,

646
00:48:13,742 --> 00:48:14,800
but they mean different things.

647
00:48:15,054 --> 00:48:19,172
We would get the same embeddings with this approach in both cases.

648
00:48:21,486 --> 00:48:22,820
So these are all limitations.

649
00:48:24,526 --> 00:48:26,212
Which is the reason why

650
00:48:27,022 --> 00:48:32,004
this is something that people have tried to resolve through different means.

651
00:48:33,408 --> 00:48:35,888
One such means is

652
00:48:37,006 --> 00:48:37,668
RNNs,

653
00:48:38,928 --> 00:48:44,676
which, as we previously saw, was the type of model that was dominating

654
00:48:45,902 --> 00:48:47,876
the models back in the 2010s.

655
00:48:49,294 --> 00:48:50,208
And so here,

656
00:48:50,958 --> 00:48:53,156
RNNs. what they do is

657
00:48:53,870 --> 00:49:00,036
that they actually have an extra quantity that they compute, which here is represented with A,

658
00:49:01,870 --> 00:49:03,492
that is also called the hidden state,

659
00:49:05,134 --> 00:49:06,884
that tries to encapsulate

660
00:49:07,726 --> 00:49:09,188
the meaning of the sentence

661
00:49:10,062 --> 00:49:11,204
seen so far.

662
00:49:12,910 --> 00:49:14,848
So we're going to see that as an example.

663
00:49:15,022 --> 00:49:18,404
So let's assume we go with the same sentence, so a cute teddy bear is reading.

664
00:49:18,926 --> 00:49:20,356
So here you would actually

665
00:49:21,742 --> 00:49:25,008
put the word A into the RNN.

666
00:49:25,486 --> 00:49:27,340
It would try to predict the word cute,

667
00:49:28,270 --> 00:49:30,756
but then what it will do is keep

668
00:49:32,334 --> 00:49:33,764
what we call a hidden state

669
00:49:34,606 --> 00:49:38,340
that encodes the meaning of the sequence seen so far

670
00:49:39,854 --> 00:49:41,284
that will be an input

671
00:49:42,638 --> 00:49:52,608
to the next prediction. So we'll have Qt as an input and then these hidden states as an input as well, in order to inform

672
00:49:53,454 --> 00:49:54,436
the next representation.

673
00:49:56,014 --> 00:49:57,124
And so on and so forth.

674
00:49:57,934 --> 00:50:03,524
So here what you're doing is you're not just considering embeddings of tokens

675
00:50:04,768 --> 00:50:05,408
on their

676
00:50:05,408 --> 00:50:08,292
own, you're also considering

677
00:50:09,422 --> 00:50:12,100
the embedding of the sequence decoded so far.

678
00:50:13,838 --> 00:50:15,364
And that allows you to

679
00:50:16,462 --> 00:50:18,020
take into consideration

680
00:50:18,926 --> 00:50:20,388
the order of the words

681
00:50:21,870 --> 00:50:22,500
in the sentence.

682
00:50:23,728 --> 00:50:29,156
And RNNs are actually models that were used in a bunch of tasks back in the day.

683
00:50:29,518 --> 00:50:32,228
So for instance, for classifications, what you would do is

684
00:50:33,006 --> 00:50:37,220
process tokens one at a time and then keep a self-mutating state

685
00:50:37,870 --> 00:50:40,676
that encodes the meaning of the sequence seen so far.

686
00:50:41,390 --> 00:50:47,008
And it would try to predict, let's say, a sentiment in the sentiment extraction case.

687
00:50:47,886 --> 00:51:00,548
In the multiclassification case, for instance, if you wanted to determine if a word was a noun or a verb, then you would actually leverage the representations corresponding to each input,

688
00:51:01,008 --> 00:51:05,060
which again would take into consideration the hidden state,

689
00:51:06,958 --> 00:51:08,036
or just generation.

690
00:51:11,182 --> 00:51:12,048
But one

691
00:51:13,648 --> 00:51:18,528
limitation of RNNs is that, okay, it was great; you would consider

692
00:51:19,598 --> 00:51:22,980
the order at which the words would appear in a sequence,

693
00:51:24,128 --> 00:51:26,436
but it would have trouble

694
00:51:27,488 --> 00:51:31,716
remembering words that happened far in the past,

695
00:51:33,326 --> 00:51:35,780
because what it used to do was to encode

696
00:51:36,590 --> 00:51:37,892
the meaning of the sequence

697
00:51:39,054 --> 00:51:40,420
in one single vector

698
00:51:41,262 --> 00:51:42,916
that kept on being modified.

699
00:51:44,398 --> 00:51:48,964
So you had this issue of what we call long-range dependency,

700
00:51:50,288 --> 00:51:50,608
where

701
00:51:51,566 --> 00:51:58,852
if you end up two sentences after something that you have talked about prior to that, you would have trouble

702
00:52:00,046 --> 00:52:04,260
teasing that out, just using the hidden state at that point.

703
00:52:06,030 --> 00:52:07,332
And that can be problematic.

704
00:52:07,854 --> 00:52:16,004
For instance, when you refer to something that happened in the past, so for instance, you can say, Oh, my teddy bear is so cute,

705
00:52:17,168 --> 00:52:17,668
it is

706
00:52:18,958 --> 00:52:19,748
three feet tall.

707
00:52:20,334 --> 00:52:20,740
So it

708
00:52:21,550 --> 00:52:23,268
refers to something that is in the past.

709
00:52:23,790 --> 00:52:25,060
So now let's assume that the

710
00:52:25,888 --> 00:52:29,220
it that I'm using is referring to something that is far in the past,

711
00:52:30,446 --> 00:52:32,688
then we have this problem of actually

712
00:52:33,486 --> 00:52:37,412
having the right set of information in the hidden state that we're considering.

713
00:52:40,206 --> 00:52:41,764
So, this is the reason why

714
00:52:42,544 --> 00:52:45,508
there were some variations of RNNs.

715
00:52:46,446 --> 00:52:51,620
One such variation was called the LSTM, Long Short-Term Memory,

716
00:52:52,430 --> 00:52:54,404
which tried to address this problem

717
00:52:55,278 --> 00:53:02,084
by not only keeping a hidden state, but also another state called the cell state,

718
00:53:03,118 --> 00:53:08,996
that was meant to carry information for longer.

719
00:53:10,766 --> 00:53:11,748
But the truth is

720
00:53:13,184 --> 00:53:13,504
even

721
00:53:14,734 --> 00:53:16,612
such variations had their limitations,

722
00:53:18,670 --> 00:53:19,460
which again

723
00:53:20,142 --> 00:53:21,220
had the same issue.

724
00:53:22,382 --> 00:53:24,836
So if we were to just recap the state of the

